course: dictionary
lesson: 13729
title: Clear enough to understand
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how easy something is to understand. Intelligible is one of them. If something is intelligible, you can follow it and make sense of it. «Her instructions were intelligible, so we built the model without any trouble.»
explain: Look at the start of the word to find its opposite. Unintelligible means impossible to understand, like a message crackling through a bad phone line. «The radio signal was so weak it was unintelligible.» The un- at the front turns the meaning the other way.
explain: Do not mix intelligible up with intelligent. Intelligent means clever and quick to learn. Intelligible is about whether speech or writing is clear enough to follow. A very clever speaker can still be unintelligible if they mumble, and a simple message can be perfectly intelligible.
fact: Intelligible means clear enough to be understood.
fact: «Make your writing intelligible» means write so that your reader can follow every idea.
fact: In science, a radio signal is intelligible when its sound can be understood clearly.
fact: The opposite of intelligible is unintelligible.
fact: Intelligent means clever, so it is a different word and should not be mixed up with intelligible.
exercise: «The message was barely intelligible through the crackly phone.» What does intelligible mean in this sentence? | Very long | Clear enough to understand | Written down in pencil | *Loud enough to hear from far away
exercise: Which word means the opposite of intelligible? | Simple | Intelligent | *Unintelligible | Clear
exercise: Which speech is intelligible? | A mumble with no clear words | *A slow, clear talk that every child in the room can follow | A talk given in a whisper so quiet nobody hears it | A talk in a language nobody in the room knows
test: «The new rules were not intelligible to anyone in the class.» What does this sentence mean? | The rules were written in gold | Everybody in the class loved the rules | The rules were very short | *Nobody in the class could understand the rules
test: A message that anyone can follow is called | intelligent | silent | unintelligible | *intelligible
exam: «The speaker's words were intelligible, so the whole audience understood her.» What does intelligible mean here? | *Clear enough to be understood | Shouting loudly | Hard to hear | Funny and silly
